reasoning:
We need to analyze the failure.

The journey steps:

1. Seek #sort-open (no assertions)
2. Press Enter -> expects attribute open="" on #sort and focus on #sort-close

Observed step failed: After pressing Enter on sort-open, the sort dialog opened (attribute open=""), but focus didn't go to #sort-close. Instead focus remained on body? Actually observed after shows focus on body (since after HTML includes both dialogs, but focus is on body). So the issue: When opening the sort drawer via Enter on button with data-bs-toggle="drawer", the focus is not moved into the drawer (specifically to the close button). According to the journey, after opening sort drawer, focus should be on #sort-close (the close button inside the drawer). But it's not happening.

Similarly for filters: after opening filters drawer via Enter on #filters-open, focus should go to #filters-search (which has autofocus). That seems to work? Not sure.

But the failure is specifically for sort drawer.

Looking at the source: The sort dialog is a <dialog class="drawer drawer-end" id="sort" aria-labelledby="sort-title">. It has no autofocus attribute on any element inside. The close button #sort-close is present.

When a dialog is opened via showModal(), focus should be moved to the first focusable element, or if the dialog has autofocus attribute, to that element. Since there is no autofocus, the UA may focus the dialog itself (which is not focusable unless tabindex added). However, the close button is focusable (button). The expectation is that focus goes to the close button.

Potential cause: The Drawer component (extends DialogBase) may not be managing focus on show. Looking at dialog-base.ts: In _showElement, they call this._element.showModal() or show(). They do not manage focus. The base class may rely on the browser's default focus behavior for <dialog>. However, the observed behavior shows focus remained on body, meaning the dialog didn't get focus. Possibly because the dialog is not modal? Let's check _getShowOptions for Drawer: overrides to return { modal: useModal, preventBodyScroll: !this._config.scroll }. useModal = Boolean(this._config.backdrop) || !this._config.scroll. For sort drawer, data-bs-toggle="drawer" data-bs-target="#sort". No data-bs-backdrop or data-bs-scroll attributes, so config defaults: backdrop: true, keyboard: true, scroll: false. So useModal = true || !false = true. So modal:true, preventBodyScroll:true. So showModal() is called.

When showModal() is called on a <dialog>, the spec says focus should be moved to the first focusable element, or if the dialog has autofocus attribute, to that element. If none, focus is set to the dialog (which is not focusable unless given tabindex=-1?). Actually the dialog element is not focusable by default; but when shown modally, the UA should move focus to the first focusable element in the dialog's tab order, or if none, to the dialog (which receives focus but is not in tab order? I'm not fully sure). However, in practice, many browsers focus the dialog itself (which is not focusable) leading to no visible focus. To ensure proper focus management, many libraries manually focus the first focusable element after opening.

